the standing tm-ghosts-plus-plus acceptance check is stale: the plugin is down to 1 warning, not 2, and the next lane to run it will get a red on a plugin nobody broke

MEASURED by lane-const on the merged tree, 2026-09-01. Master and lane report
identically, so this is fully attributed to master and is NOT a lane regression:

  lsp-base3:  src/HookHelper.as:49:31: warning: Signed/Unsigned mismatch -> 1 diagnostics
  lsp-fixed2: src/HookHelper.as:49:31: warning: Signed/Unsigned mismatch -> 1 diagnostics

`src/Scrubber.as:299:47` has disappeared. It went somewhere in B155 / B156 /
B158; B155's `Math::Min(uint(20), buf.Length)` fix is the obvious candidate but
that is a hypothesis, not a measurement. B152's fleet A/B independently proved
base == fixed across all 152 plugins, which is what rules out the lane.

WHY THIS NEEDS A TICKET RATHER THAN A SHRUG. "tm-ghosts-plus-plus reports 0
errors and its 2 signed/unsigned warnings survive" has been the standing
acceptance check since B144, and it is quoted in lane briefs. The next lane to
run it gets a red on a plugin nobody broke -- and the likely response to an
unexplained red on a standing check is to go looking in the lane's own diff,
which is where the time goes.

TWO PIECES OF WORK, and the second is the one that matters:

1. Attribute the disappearance to a specific commit (bisect across B155/B156/B158
   is cheap -- three candidates) and confirm the remaining warning is the one we
   expect to keep. If Scrubber.as:299 was a false positive that B155 correctly
   killed, that is a WIN and should be recorded as one, not quietly absorbed.

2. Update the standing check wherever it is written down, and note the date and
   the engine version alongside the number. A bare count with no provenance is
   exactly the kind of remembered figure that goes stale silently -- see
   docs/verification-hazards.md, "predict the count before the run", and the two
   count errors made in this batch (12 ignored suite-wide read as per-harness;
   ten backlog commits read as ten merges). A fleet acceptance number is the same
   shape and rots the same way.

Do not simply change 2 to 1. A number with no provenance rots again in a week.